-- allium: 3
--
-- Technique — what a person can deduce from a grid
--
-- Scope
--   The named techniques a person solves Sudoku by, stated once: when each
--   holds on a grid of digits and candidates, what it places or strikes, and
--   what it asks of whoever would find it. And the profile of a player, which
--   says how much of that one person can find. It is the ground four models
--   of a player stand on, none of which imports another. reach.allium has a
--   limit hide a deduction, effort.allium has it make one dear, and
--   lapse.allium has it lead a player into guessing; human-solving.allium has
--   it change what is noticed, kept in mind and got wrong, by chance. None of
--   them says again what a hidden pair is. The first three read the Profile
--   and keep the Grid stated here. human-solving.allium shares the catalogue
--   and its proofs, keeps its own sheet and mind in place of the Grid, owns a
--   finer description of a player, and states the projection from that
--   description to this Profile, so that one player can be put through all
--   four.
--   Sources: docs/explanation/solving-sudoku.md, for the techniques, their
--   names and the order a careful solver tries them in, and Cowan (2001),
--   "The magical number 4 in short-term memory", Behavioral and Brain
--   Sciences 24, for how much is held in mind at once.
--
-- Includes
--   - The supported catalogue in ladder order, with
--     its family, whether it reads marks, and what it loads a mind with. The
--     ladder is a conventional comparison order, not a cognitive law.
--   - The grid a technique is read from: cells with digits and candidates,
--     and the units they sit in. One grid serves a set of givens and a puzzle
--     in play alike, and a model may keep more than one.
--   - Twenty-nine techniques, through basic fish, wings, single digit
--     patterns, colouring, bounded chains and two uniqueness patterns.
--     Catalogue guarantees pin their exact supported variants and proofs.
--   - Deductions: one for each place a technique holds, saying where, with
--     which digits, and what it places or strikes.
--   - The profile: repertoire, capacity, spans, marking, order, fixation,
--     upkeep, budget, fatigue and patience, and two presets, novice and
--     expert. reach, effort and lapse each read the fields they need;
--     human-solving.allium projects its own description onto them.
--
-- Excludes
--   - Which deduction a player takes, what a missed one costs, and when a
--     player guesses. Those are the models', and each says so itself.
--   - Keeping candidates true. A grid is laid out with every candidate the
--     start allows and no other. After that a placed digit leaves its peers'
--     candidates only when the grid's keeper strikes it; this module says
--     which candidates are stale and no more, because how well marks are
--     kept is the very thing lapse.allium varies.
--   - Finned, sashimi and other complex fish, grouped AICs, almost locked
--     sets, forcing nets, other uniqueness patterns and unrestricted Nishio.
--     These are deferred, not aliases for the supported patterns.
--   - Chance. Nothing here is drawn at random; a profile is a fixed set of
--     figures and the same grid licenses the same deductions every time.
--   - How a grid is stored, how deductions are found quickly, and the order
--     in which equal deductions are listed.
--   - Variants, as sudoku.allium excludes them.
--
-- Dependencies
--   sudoku.allium, for Given, Position and the two sides of the grid. It does
--   not depend on solver.allium: a technique strikes only what the rules
--   forbid, which DeductionsAreSound states against sudoku.allium's
--   solution, and no search is run to find one.
--
-- Vocabulary
--   sudoku.allium's and solver.allium's words stand: given, unit (never
--   house), peer, box, candidate, guess, contradiction. To them: technique.
--   Deduction, one place a technique holds. Placement, a digit a deduction
--   puts in a cell; strike, candidates it removes (never eliminate). Mark, a
--   candidate as the player has it written. Load, how many things a deduction
--   has held in mind at once; capacity, how many a player can. Extent, how
--   much of the grid a deduction is read across; span, an extent a player
--   takes in. Pointing and claiming, never box-line reduction. Cross-hatch, a
--   hidden single read from the placed digits alone, in a box or, by the same
--   crossing-out, in a line. Subset for a pair, a triple or a quad.
--   For the patterns past subsets: bivalue, of an open cell with two
--   candidates; conjugate pair, an unplaced digit's only two places in a
--   unit. Link, two candidates joined weakly (at most one is true) or
--   strongly (at least one is); literal, a candidate asserted or denied;
--   path, literals each following from the last by one link. Base and
--   cover, the two sets of lines a fish is read across. Proof, the units,
--   links and paths a deduction rests on; witness, one particular place a
--   technique holds, with its proof, as against the technique holding
--   somewhere.

use "./sudoku.allium" as sudoku

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum UnitKind { row | column | box }

-- The source's families, in the order its workflow reaches them.
enum Family {
    singles | locked_candidates | naked_subsets | hidden_subsets
    | fish | wings | colouring | chains | uniqueness | contradiction
}

-- How much of the grid a deduction is read across, narrowest first: a cell's
-- own marks; one box; one row or column; a box and a line; several units.
enum Extent { one_cell | one_box | one_line | box_and_line | multiple_units }

enum LinkKind { weak | strong }

-- Whether the player writes candidates down. A player who does not has only
-- the techniques that read placed digits.
enum Marking { no_marks | full_marks }

-- Systematic is the source's fixed-point workflow: the lowest technique on
-- the ladder that yields anything, and back to the bottom after every step.
-- Unsystematic takes what it sees, low or high.
enum Order { systematic | unsystematic }

-- What a player's eye returns to: nothing in particular, the cells around
-- the last step, or the digit the last step dealt in.
enum Fixation { unfixed | nearby | same_digit }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Whatever keeps a grid: a model's run. A model passes its own entity here,
-- so that it can find the grid it opened and this module need not know any
-- model.
external entity Keeper {}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value UnitRef {
    kind: UnitKind

    -- From 1 to config.side. Rows count from the top, columns from the left
    -- and boxes left to right and then top to bottom, as GridCell.box_number.
    index: Integer
}

-- A digit a deduction puts in a cell.
value Placement {
    position: sudoku/Position
    digit: Integer
}

-- The candidates a deduction removes from one cell.
value Strike {
    position: sudoku/Position
    digits: Set<Integer>
}

-- A candidate proposition: this cell holds this digit. A literal states
-- that proposition or its negation. Links use the unchanged input grid.
value CandidateRef {
    position: sudoku/Position
    digit: Integer
}

value Literal {
    candidate: CandidateRef
    positive: Boolean
}

value Link {
    left: CandidateRef
    right: CandidateRef
    kind: LinkKind
}

value InferencePath {
    literals: List<Literal>
}

value Proof {
    -- Includes every unit whose full candidate coverage is used, not just
    -- units containing a striking target. Singles/subsets need no links.
    units: Set<UnitRef>
    links: Set<Link>
    paths: List<InferencePath>
}

-- One place a technique holds on a grid. licensed below says which exist;
-- the fields are pinned there, technique by technique.
value Deduction {
    technique: Technique

    -- The primary unit for the first twelve techniques except the naked
    -- single, which has none; absent for the patterns spanning several
    -- units.
    unit: UnitRef?

    extent: Extent

    -- Where the pattern sits and the digits it is made of.
    cells: Set<sudoku/Position>
    digits: Set<Integer>

    -- A deduction places or strikes and never both. A placed digit leaves
    -- its peers' candidates by upkeep, which is the keeper's and not a
    -- strike of the deduction's.
    placement: Placement?
    strikes: Set<Strike>

    load: Integer
    proof: Proof
}

-- A player. Every figure is a field and none is config, because config in
-- these modules names a number and does not tune one; a player is tuned.
-- Each model reads the fields its header names and ignores the rest, so one
-- profile can be put through all four and the results compared.
value Profile {
    -- The techniques the player knows.
    repertoire: Set<Technique>

    -- A coarse load ceiling. Cowan's conditional chunk estimates motivate
    -- testing small capacities; they do not measure Sudoku cells or fix an
    -- individual's capacity. human-solving.allium counts retained facts and
    -- chunks instead.
    capacity: Integer

    -- The extents the player takes in. A set and not a ceiling, so that a
    -- player can be given a line and not a box, or the reverse.
    spans: Set<Extent>

    marking: Marking
    order: Order
    fixation: Fixation

    -- The kinds of unit in which the player strikes a digit from their marks
    -- after placing it. lapse.allium's; the other models keep every mark
    -- true.
    upkeep: Set<UnitKind>

    -- How much effort the player will spend on one puzzle, and how much of
    -- it takes one from capacity. effort.allium's unit, and provisional with
    -- it.
    budget: Integer
    fatigue: Integer

    -- How many looks in a row may find nothing before the player guesses.
    -- lapse.allium's, which checks lapsed marks while patience is left and
    -- guesses sooner where none has lapsed, so that there it is a switch:
    -- none or some. reach.allium and effort.allium never guess.
    patience: Integer

    knows(x): x.technique in repertoire
    holds(x): x.load <= capacity
    takes_in(x): x.extent in spans
    can_read(x): not x.technique.reads_marks or marking = full_marks

    -- What no part of the profile hides. Each model reads this as its own
    -- seeing or being within, and none restates it.
    sees(x): knows(x) and holds(x) and takes_in(x) and can_read(x)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- An entry in the catalogue. The techniques are the default instances below
-- and no rule makes another.
entity Technique {
    -- Its place on the ladder, 1 the first thing tried. No two share one.
    rank: Integer

    family: Family

    -- Whether it reads candidates. One that does not reads placed digits
    -- alone and is open to a player who keeps no marks.
    reads_marks: Boolean

    -- What one of its deductions holds in mind at once, counted in chunks
    -- and not in cells and digits: a single is one thing, pointing and
    -- claiming are a box and a line, and a subset of k cells is k. Absent
    -- where it depends on the deduction or has not been decided.
    load: Integer?

    -- Whether this module says when it holds. Only a specified technique is
    -- ever licensed.
    is_specified: Boolean
}

entity Grid {
    keeper: Keeper

    -- An explicit premise supplied by the keeper, never inferred from the
    -- apparent shape. False disables both uniqueness techniques. A hint
    -- over unverified player entries cannot assert this premise.
    uniqueness_promised: Boolean

    -- The digits standing in the grid when it is opened. For a set of givens
    -- that is the givens. For a puzzle in play it is every digit in it, the
    -- player's read as fixed like the setter's.
    start: Set<sudoku/Given>

    cells: GridCell with grid = this
    units: Unit with grid = this
    open_cells: cells where digit = null
    stale_cells: cells where has_stale_candidate

    is_laid_out: cells.count = config.side * config.side
    is_filled: is_laid_out and open_cells.count = 0

    -- No candidate stands that a placed peer rules out. Every technique
    -- below is stated of candidates as they stand, kept or not.
    is_kept: is_laid_out and stale_cells.count = 0

    -- An open cell with nothing left. It cannot happen while the candidates
    -- are true; lapse.allium is where they may not be.
    is_contradictory: open_cells.any(c => c.candidates.count = 0)

    -- licensed is a black box, pinned by EveryDeductionIsLicensed and by
    -- this. It holds one deduction for each of the following and no other,
    -- each once, and the same ones whenever the grid is the same. A digit
    -- "has a place" in an open cell that allows it.
    --   full_unit: each unit with one open cell. It places in that cell the
    --     one digit the unit has not placed. Reads no marks.
    --   cross_hatch: each unit and digit that cross_hatches. It places the
    --     digit in the one open cell no placed peer rules out. Reads no marks.
    --   naked_single: each open cell with one candidate. It places it.
    --   hidden_single: each unit and digit that hides_single. It places the
    --     digit in its one place.
    --   pointing: each box, digit and line that point. It strikes the digit
    --     from every open cell of the line outside the box.
    --   claiming: each line and digit that claim. It strikes the digit from
    --     every open cell of the box outside the line.
    --   naked pair, triple, quad: each unit and set of k open cells that
    --     is_naked_subset, k being the technique's load. It strikes the
    --     set's digits from every other open cell of the unit.
    --   hidden pair, triple, quad: each unit, set of k digits and set of k
    --     open cells that is_hidden_subset. It strikes every other digit
    --     from those cells.
    -- A deduction's cells are the pattern's cells and its digits the
    -- pattern's digits; it has at most one strike to a position, and every
    -- strike names only candidates that stand. One placement may be licensed
    -- by several techniques, and then each has its own deduction.
    -- Advanced patterns and every proof/output are additionally constrained
    -- by Catalogue below. witness_matches is true exactly when all those
    -- obligations hold for this grid and this deduction, including the
    -- first twelve techniques; a pattern elsewhere cannot license x.
    deductions: licensed(this)
}

entity Unit {
    grid: Grid
    kind: UnitKind
    index: Integer

    cells: GridCell with grid = this.grid
        and ((this.kind = row and row = this.index)
            or (this.kind = column and column = this.index)
            or (this.kind = box and box_number = this.index))
    open_cells: cells where digit = null
    placed_digits: cells where digit != null -> digit

    -- Full unit ---------------------------------------------

    -- One cell left, so one digit left, and it goes there.
    is_full_unit: open_cells.count = 1

    -- Singles -----------------------------------------------

    -- The digit is still to be placed here and placed digits rule it out of
    -- every open cell but one. Read from the grid as printed.
    cross_hatches(d):
        not (d in placed_digits)
            and open_cells.any(c => c.is_open_to(d)
                and open_cells.all(o => o = c or not o.is_open_to(d)))

    -- The same, read from candidates: one cell of the unit allows the digit,
    -- whatever else it allows.
    hides_single(d):
        not (d in placed_digits)
            and open_cells.any(c => c.allows(d)
                and open_cells.all(o => o = c or not o.allows(d)))

    -- Pointing and claiming ---------------------------------

    -- Two places or more, for a digit still to be placed here. With one it
    -- is a hidden single. A digit the unit has placed has no places,
    -- whatever stale marks still allow: without this, pointing and claiming
    -- would strike on an unkept grid what the solution needs.
    has_places(d):
        not (d in placed_digits)
            and open_cells.any(a => open_cells.any(b => a != b and a.allows(d) and b.allows(d)))

    -- A box whose places for a digit lie in one row, which allows the digit
    -- somewhere outside the box. The box must hold the digit, so it holds it
    -- on that row, and the rest of the row cannot.
    points_along_row(d):
        kind = box and has_places(d)
            and open_cells.all(a => open_cells.all(b =>
                a.allows(d) and b.allows(d) implies a.row = b.row))
            and open_cells.any(a => a.allows(d)
                and a.row_mates.any(m => not (m in cells) and m.allows(d)))

    points_along_column(d):
        kind = box and has_places(d)
            and open_cells.all(a => open_cells.all(b =>
                a.allows(d) and b.allows(d) implies a.column = b.column))
            and open_cells.any(a => a.allows(d)
                and a.column_mates.any(m => not (m in cells) and m.allows(d)))

    points(d): points_along_row(d) or points_along_column(d)

    -- The converse. A line whose places for a digit lie in one box, which
    -- allows the digit somewhere off the line.
    claims(d):
        kind != box and has_places(d)
            and open_cells.all(a => open_cells.all(b =>
                a.allows(d) and b.allows(d) implies a.box_number = b.box_number))
            and open_cells.any(a => a.allows(d)
                and a.box_mates.any(m => not (m in cells) and m.allows(d)))

    -- Subsets -----------------------------------------------

    -- Open cells of this unit that between them allow exactly as many digits
    -- as there are cells. Those digits go in those cells, so no other cell of
    -- the unit holds one; and some other cell allows one, or nothing is
    -- struck. candidates_across is a black box: every digit some cell of the
    -- set allows, each once.
    is_naked_subset(s):
        s.all(c => c in open_cells)
            and candidates_across(s).count = s.count
            and open_cells.any(c => not (c in s)
                and c.candidates.any(d => d in candidates_across(s)))

    -- Digits still to be placed in this unit, as many as the cells, whose
    -- every place lies in those cells. Those cells hold those digits, so they
    -- hold nothing else; and one of them allows something else, or nothing is
    -- struck.
    is_hidden_subset(ds, s):
        ds.count = s.count
            and s.all(c => c in open_cells)
            and ds.all(d => not (d in placed_digits) and s.any(c => c.allows(d)))
            and open_cells.all(c => c in s or not c.candidates.any(d => d in ds))
            and s.any(c => c.candidates.any(d => not (d in ds)))

    -- cell_subsets is a black box: every set of that many of the cells, each
    -- once. digit_subsets likewise, of the digits from 1 to its first
    -- argument.
    has_naked_subset(k): cell_subsets(open_cells, k).any(s => is_naked_subset(s))

    has_hidden_subset(k):
        digit_subsets(config.side, k).any(ds =>
            cell_subsets(open_cells, k).any(s => is_hidden_subset(ds, s)))

    -- Licensing ---------------------------------------------

    -- Whether a deduction read in this unit is one a technique allows.
    licenses(x):
        x.unit != null and x.unit.kind = kind and x.unit.index = index
            and ((x.technique = full_unit and is_full_unit)
                or (x.technique = cross_hatch and x.digits.any(d => cross_hatches(d)))
                or (x.technique = hidden_single and x.digits.any(d => hides_single(d)))
                or (x.technique = pointing and x.digits.any(d => points(d)))
                or (x.technique = claiming and x.digits.any(d => claims(d)))
                or (x.technique.family = naked_subsets and has_naked_subset(x.load))
                or (x.technique.family = hidden_subsets and has_hidden_subset(x.load)))
}

entity GridCell {
    grid: Grid
    row: Integer
    column: Integer
    band: Integer
    stack: Integer

    -- Absent until the cell is placed: by the start or by a step.
    digit: Integer?

    -- The digits the cell may still hold, as its keeper has them. A placed
    -- cell keeps its digit as its one candidate.
    candidates: Set<Integer>

    -- Filled by the start, and so not by any step.
    is_start: grid.start.any(g => g.position.row = row and g.position.column = column)

    -- Boxes count left to right and then top to bottom, from 1.
    box_number: (band - 1) * config.box_side + stack

    row_mates: GridCell with grid = this.grid
        and row = this.row and column != this.column
    column_mates: GridCell with grid = this.grid
        and column = this.column and row != this.row
    box_mates: GridCell with grid = this.grid
        and band = this.band and stack = this.stack
        and (row != this.row or column != this.column)

    -- As sudoku.allium's Cell.peers, within one grid.
    peers: GridCell with grid = this.grid
        and (row = this.row or column = this.column or (band = this.band and stack = this.stack))
        and (row != this.row or column != this.column)

    placed_peer_digits: peers where digit != null -> digit

    -- By sight: open, and no placed peer holds the digit. It asks nothing of
    -- the candidates, so it is true of a grid nobody has marked.
    is_open_to(d): digit = null and not (d in placed_peer_digits)

    -- By marks: open, and the digit is among its candidates.
    allows(d): digit = null and d in candidates

    -- A candidate a placed peer rules out. Striking it is the keeper's.
    has_stale_candidate: candidates.any(d => d in placed_peer_digits)

    is_naked_single: digit = null and candidates.count = 1

    licenses(x):
        x.technique = naked_single and x.unit = null and is_naked_single
            and x.placement != null
            and x.placement.position.row = row
            and x.placement.position.column = column
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- sudoku.allium's two figures, under its names, restated as
    -- solver.allium restates them.
    box_side: Integer = sudoku/config.box_side
    side: Integer = sudoku/config.side

    -- Finite catalogue proof limits, independent of a player's smaller
    -- reasoning/memory limits. Changing either changes catalogue_version.
    max_chain_links: Integer = 24
    max_forcing_paths: Integer = 9

    -- Names this catalogue: which techniques are specified, their exact
    -- variants and the two limits above. A model that records what it was
    -- run against, as human-solving.allium does, records this. It moves when
    -- any of them does and not when a comment or a profile preset does.
    catalogue_version: String = "technique-2"
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Defaults
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The catalogue, in ladder order: the order the source's workflow tries
-- things in, cheap and local before dear and global. By sight before by
-- marks; singles, then pointing and claiming, then subsets by size with
-- naked before hidden; then fish, wings, single-digit patterns, colouring,
-- chains, bounded forcing and uniqueness.

default Technique full_unit = {
    rank: 1, family: singles, reads_marks: false, load: 1, is_specified: true
}

default Technique cross_hatch = {
    rank: 2, family: singles, reads_marks: false, load: 1, is_specified: true
}

default Technique naked_single = {
    rank: 3, family: singles, reads_marks: true, load: 1, is_specified: true
}

default Technique hidden_single = {
    rank: 4, family: singles, reads_marks: true, load: 1, is_specified: true
}

default Technique pointing = {
    rank: 5, family: locked_candidates, reads_marks: true, load: 2, is_specified: true
}

default Technique claiming = {
    rank: 6, family: locked_candidates, reads_marks: true, load: 2, is_specified: true
}

default Technique naked_pair = {
    rank: 7, family: naked_subsets, reads_marks: true, load: 2, is_specified: true
}

default Technique hidden_pair = {
    rank: 8, family: hidden_subsets, reads_marks: true, load: 2, is_specified: true
}

default Technique naked_triple = {
    rank: 9, family: naked_subsets, reads_marks: true, load: 3, is_specified: true
}

default Technique hidden_triple = {
    rank: 10, family: hidden_subsets, reads_marks: true, load: 3, is_specified: true
}

default Technique naked_quad = {
    rank: 11, family: naked_subsets, reads_marks: true, load: 4, is_specified: true
}

default Technique hidden_quad = {
    rank: 12, family: hidden_subsets, reads_marks: true, load: 4, is_specified: true
}

-- These loads are provisional costs, not measurements of human working
-- memory. A variable load is the number of distinct candidate propositions
-- in the proof (at least one). human-solving.allium uses explicit retained
-- facts and its own learned chunks instead of this shortcut.

default Technique x_wing = {
    rank: 13, family: fish, reads_marks: true, load: 2, is_specified: true
}

default Technique swordfish = {
    rank: 14, family: fish, reads_marks: true, load: 3, is_specified: true
}

default Technique jellyfish = {
    rank: 15, family: fish, reads_marks: true, load: 4, is_specified: true
}

default Technique xy_wing = {
    rank: 16, family: wings, reads_marks: true, load: 3, is_specified: true
}

default Technique xyz_wing = {
    rank: 17, family: wings, reads_marks: true, load: 3, is_specified: true
}

default Technique w_wing = {
    rank: 18, family: wings, reads_marks: true, load: 4, is_specified: true
}

default Technique skyscraper = {
    rank: 19, family: chains, reads_marks: true, load: 4, is_specified: true
}

default Technique two_string_kite = {
    rank: 20, family: chains, reads_marks: true, load: 4, is_specified: true
}

default Technique empty_rectangle = {
    rank: 21, family: chains, reads_marks: true, load: null, is_specified: true
}

default Technique simple_colouring = {
    rank: 22, family: colouring, reads_marks: true, load: null, is_specified: true
}

default Technique remote_pair = {
    rank: 23, family: chains, reads_marks: true, load: null, is_specified: true
}

default Technique x_chain = {
    rank: 24, family: chains, reads_marks: true, load: null, is_specified: true
}

default Technique xy_chain = {
    rank: 25, family: chains, reads_marks: true, load: null, is_specified: true
}

default Technique alternating_inference_chain = {
    rank: 26, family: chains, reads_marks: true, load: null, is_specified: true
}

default Technique forcing_chain = {
    rank: 27, family: contradiction, reads_marks: true, load: null, is_specified: true
}

default Technique unique_rectangle_type_1 = {
    rank: 28, family: uniqueness, reads_marks: true, load: 4, is_specified: true
}

default Technique bug_plus_one = {
    rank: 29, family: uniqueness, reads_marks: true, load: null, is_specified: true
}

-- Two presets, one at each end. Both are ordinary profiles, and anything
-- between them is made by moving a field.

-- Someone who knows the rules and has been shown how to scan. No marks, so
-- the two singles that read them are known and out of reach; takes what is
-- seen and stays near it; soon guesses.
default Profile novice = {
    repertoire: { full_unit, cross_hatch, naked_single, hidden_single },
    capacity: 3,
    spans: { one_cell, one_box, one_line },
    marking: no_marks,
    order: unsystematic,
    fixation: nearby,
    upkeep: { box },
    budget: 200,
    fatigue: 50,
    patience: 3
}

-- Someone who knows the supported catalogue. Their capacity may still hide
-- a long proof; knowledge and capacity remain distinct.
default Profile expert = {
    repertoire: {
        full_unit, cross_hatch, naked_single, hidden_single,
        pointing, claiming,
        naked_pair, hidden_pair, naked_triple, hidden_triple, naked_quad, hidden_quad,
        x_wing, swordfish, jellyfish, xy_wing, xyz_wing,
        w_wing, skyscraper, two_string_kite, empty_rectangle, simple_colouring,
        remote_pair, x_chain, xy_chain, alternating_inference_chain,
        forcing_chain, unique_rectangle_type_1, bug_plus_one
    },
    capacity: 4,
    spans: { one_cell, one_box, one_line, box_and_line, multiple_units },
    marking: full_marks,
    order: systematic,
    fixation: unfixed,
    upkeep: { row, column, box },
    budget: 2000,
    fatigue: 500,
    patience: 50
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

rule LayOutGrid {
    when: grid: Grid.created

    -- A grid, its cells and its units come into being together, as
    -- sudoku.allium's puzzle and its cells do. grid_positions, box_index and
    -- given_digit_at are sudoku.allium's black boxes and digits_up_to is
    -- solver.allium's, read as they are there. start_digits_seen_from is a
    -- black box: the digit of every entry of the start whose position shares
    -- a row, a column or a box with this one and is not this one. A cell the
    -- start fills is placed from the beginning, and every other cell allows
    -- what the start does not rule out. So a grid is born kept: no candidate
    -- is stale until a placement makes it so, and the first marking-up is
    -- nobody's to get wrong.
    ensures:
        for position in grid_positions(config.side):
            let start_digit = given_digit_at(grid.start, position)
            GridCell.created(
                grid: grid,
                row: position.row,
                column: position.column,
                band: box_index(position.row, config.box_side),
                stack: box_index(position.column, config.box_side),
                digit: start_digit,
                candidates:
                    if start_digit != null: { start_digit }
                    else: digits_up_to(config.side) - start_digits_seen_from(grid.start, position)
            )
        for index in digits_up_to(config.side):
            Unit.created(grid: grid, kind: row, index: index)
            Unit.created(grid: grid, kind: column, index: index)
            Unit.created(grid: grid, kind: box, index: index)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- These hold in every state a grid can reach, whoever keeps it. A state is
-- what stands once a stimulus and the rules it sets off have run, as in
-- sudoku.allium, and a grid is created by one rule and laid out by the next.

invariant EveryDeductionIsLicensed {
    for grid in Grids:
        grid.deductions.all(x =>
            witness_matches(grid, x)
                and (grid.units.any(u => u.licenses(x))
                    or grid.cells.any(c => c.licenses(x))
                    or x.technique.rank > hidden_quad.rank))
}

invariant OnlySpecifiedTechniquesAreLicensed {
    for grid in Grids:
        grid.deductions.all(x => x.technique.is_specified)
}

-- A deduction changes the grid, so taking one is progress, and it is one
-- thing or the other.
invariant ADeductionPlacesOrStrikes {
    for grid in Grids:
        grid.deductions.all(x =>
            (x.placement != null and x.strikes.count = 0)
                or (x.placement = null and x.strikes.count > 0))
}

invariant LoadIsTheTechniques {
    for grid in Grids:
        grid.deductions.all(x => x.technique.load != null implies x.load = x.technique.load)
}

-- A cell's own marks for a naked single; a box and a line for pointing and
-- claiming; several units for advanced patterns; otherwise the one unit.
-- A cross-hatch
-- looks along the lines through its unit for the digit, and is still read in
-- the one unit: following a line to a printed digit is not holding the line.
invariant ExtentFollowsThePattern {
    for grid in Grids:
        grid.deductions.all(x =>
            (x.technique = naked_single implies x.extent = one_cell)
                and (x.technique.family = locked_candidates implies x.extent = box_and_line)
                and (x.technique.rank > hidden_quad.rank implies
                    x.unit = null and x.extent = multiple_units)
                and (x.unit != null and x.technique.family != locked_candidates implies
                    (x.unit.kind = box implies x.extent = one_box)
                        and (x.unit.kind != box implies x.extent = one_line)))
}

invariant RanksAreDistinct {
    for a in Techniques:
        for b in Techniques:
            a != b implies a.rank != b.rank
}

-- By-sight techniques ask less than their by-marks twins and come first, so
-- a systematic player who could use either is recorded as using the cheaper.
invariant SightComesBeforeMarks {
    for a in Techniques:
        for b in Techniques:
            a.family = singles and b.family = singles
                and not a.reads_marks and b.reads_marks
                implies a.rank < b.rank
}

invariant PlacedCellsKeepOnlyTheirDigit {
    for cell in GridCells:
        cell.digit != null implies cell.candidates.all(d => d = cell.digit)
}

-- What LayOutGrid establishes, and what makes has_stale_candidate something
-- that only ever turns true after a placement.
invariant AGridIsBornKept {
    for grid in Grids:
        grid.is_laid_out and grid.cells.all(c => c.digit = null or c.is_start) implies grid.is_kept
}

invariant CandidatesAreDigits {
    for cell in GridCells:
        cell.candidates.all(d => d >= 1 and d <= config.side)
}

invariant EveryUnitIsWhole {
    for unit in Units:
        unit.grid.is_laid_out implies unit.cells.count = config.side
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a model reads: the catalogue, the grid and what the grid licenses.
-- It provides nothing, because a grid is opened and changed by its keeper.
surface Catalogue {
    context grid: Grid

    exposes:
        grid.keeper
        grid.uniqueness_promised
        grid.is_filled
        grid.is_kept
        grid.is_contradictory
        grid.deductions
        for cell in grid.cells:
            cell.row
            cell.column
            cell.box_number
            cell.digit
            cell.candidates
            cell.has_stale_candidate
        for technique in Techniques:
            technique.rank
            technique.family
            technique.reads_marks
            technique.load
            technique.is_specified

    @guarantee DeductionsAreSound
        -- Of a grid whose start is part of a solution, whose placed digits
        -- are that solution's and whose every open cell still allows the
        -- solution's digit: every licensed deduction places the solution's
        -- digit or strikes only digits the solution does not use there. A
        -- solution is a way to fill the grid that sudoku.allium's
        -- solution_count counts. So a keeper that takes only licensed
        -- deductions from a true grid is never wrong, and one whose marks
        -- have lost a true candidate has no such promise. A uniqueness
        -- deduction additionally requires a truthful uniqueness_promised:
        -- the original givens have exactly one solution. The promise is a
        -- premise, never a request to search for a solution during a proof.

    @guarantee ExtraCandidatesHideAndNeverMislead
        -- Every technique here is read from what the candidates rule out.
        -- Extra candidates may hide patterns, but need not yield a strict
        -- subset of another grid's catalogue: changed shapes can expose a
        -- different valid proof. They cannot make a deduction unsound under
        -- DeductionsAreSound's premises. This is a soundness guarantee, not
        -- a monotonicity claim about which patterns are recognized.

    @guarantee SameGridSameDeductions
        -- The same digits and candidates license the same deductions, every
        -- time, with the same uniqueness premise and catalogue limits.
        -- Nothing here is left to chance.

    @guarantee ProofsMatchTheirOutputs
        -- witness_matches checks the particular supporting cells, digits,
        -- units, links and paths of x against the unchanged input grid.
        -- Every candidate mentioned is in 1..9 at a valid position. Every
        -- target is open; placements have a standing candidate (or, for a
        -- by-sight single, are allowed by placed peers). Every strike is
        -- nonempty, removes standing candidates, and follows THIS witness.
        -- It cannot borrow a matching pattern elsewhere on the board.
        -- The first twelve patterns have exactly the outputs listed under
        -- Grid.deductions. Advanced patterns strike every eligible target
        -- for that witness. Placement proofs yield one deduction per forced
        -- placement; strikes and placements are never combined in x.
        -- Proof.units includes the full units used for absence/count claims.
        -- A complete candidate set is required to assert an absence.
        -- Reading sparse marks as a complete set is not a valid proof.
        -- x.cells and x.digits are the core pattern, not unrelated targets.
        -- Variable load counts its distinct candidate propositions; colour
        -- and BUG proofs include all their supporting candidate propositions.
        -- Duplicate witnesses from reversed paths or swapped labels have one
        -- canonical representation. Different valid technique names remain
        -- separate deductions, not separate credit for one applied move.

    @guarantee BasicFish
        -- X-Wing, Swordfish and Jellyfish use k=2,3,4 respectively.
        -- Choose k distinct base rows and k distinct cover columns, or
        -- transpose both. In each base d is unplaced and has 2..k places;
        -- their union uses exactly those k covers. Strike d in the covers
        -- outside the bases. No fins, boxes-as-lines or hidden exceptions.
        -- Proof.units lists all bases and covers. With no target no deduction
        -- exists. Reducible patterns are allowed; ladder order is separate.

    @guarantee Wings
        -- XY-Wing: distinct a,b,c; pivot {a,b}, two peer wings {a,c}
        -- and {b,c}. Strike c from other cells seeing both wings.
        -- XYZ-Wing changes the pivot to {a,b,c}; targets must additionally
        -- see the pivot. Each named set is the cell's complete candidate set.
        -- W-Wing: two nonpeer {a,b} cells P,Q; a conjugate pair U,V
        -- for a in one unit, all four cells distinct. P sees U and Q sees V.
        -- Strike b in other cells seeing P and Q. Swapping a and b is a
        -- separate witness. Merely sharing a candidate is insufficient.

    @guarantee SingleDigitPatterns
        -- Skyscraper: two parallel lines each have exactly two places for
        -- unplaced d. One endpoint from each lies on the same perpendicular
        -- line; the other endpoints lie on different perpendicular lines.
        -- Strike d from other cells seeing both unaligned endpoints.
        -- Two-String Kite: a row conjugate pair and a column conjugate pair
        -- for d, four distinct cells; one endpoint from each shares a box.
        -- Strike d from other cells seeing both remaining endpoints.
        -- Empty Rectangle: all places of unplaced d in a box lie in row R or
        -- column C; both arms are nonempty, their intersection lacks d, at
        -- least three places exist. Outside the box, P=(R,K), Q=(S,K) form a
        -- column conjugate pair for d. Strike d at (S,C) outside the box.
        -- Also transpose this construction. The two-place variant is
        -- deferred.

    @guarantee LinkMeaning
        -- A node is a standing candidate. Distinct nodes are weakly linked
        -- exactly when they are different digits in one cell or the same
        -- digit in peer cells: at most one is true. A strong link requires
        -- exactly two candidates in one open cell or exactly two places for
        -- an unplaced digit in one unit: at least one is true. These links
        -- are also weak; a weak link is not necessarily strong.
        -- A weak link licenses positive(left) -> negative(right); a strong
        -- link licenses negative(left) -> positive(right), both directions.
        -- Each path step must be one such implication using the input grid.
        -- No grouped nodes, links discovered by an oracle, or deductions
        -- depending on accumulated hypothetical changes are admitted.

    @guarantee SimpleColouring
        -- For one digit, two-colour a connected component of conjugate
        -- links; each link joins opposite labels. A same-label peer pair
        -- makes that label false: strike d at all its nodes (colour wrap).
        -- An outside node seeing both labels loses d (colour trap).
        -- Disconnected components cannot share a label assignment as proof.
        -- Labels are logical values, not a requirement to display colour.

    @guarantee RemotePairs
        -- At least four distinct cells each have exactly {a,b}. Consecutive
        -- cells are peers, so their assignments alternate. Two cells an odd
        -- number of links apart have opposite assignments. Strike both a
        -- and b, where present, from outside cells seeing such a pair.
        -- At most config.max_chain_links peer links; no repeating cells.

    @guarantee AlternatingChains
        -- For endpoint strikes, candidate nodes alternate strong and weak links,
        -- starting and ending strong, with at most config.max_chain_links.
        -- It proves that at least one endpoint is true. Strike any candidate
        -- weakly linked to BOTH endpoints, excluding the endpoints.
        -- X-Chain restricts all nodes to one digit, with at least three links.
        -- XY-Chain uses only bivalue cells: strong links within cells,
        -- weak links between peers on a common digit, equal endpoint digits,
        -- at least three distinct cells.
        -- Alternating Inference Chain permits both sorts of single-node
        -- strong link and different endpoint digits. This includes the
        -- usual same-digit and different-digit endpoint strikes.
        -- Also permit a path from a literal to its negation: strike its
        -- candidate if the start is positive, place it if negative.
        -- These closure paths obey LinkMeaning: a positive start uses a
        -- weak first link, a negative start a strong one. The same path
        -- length bound applies; the endpoint-strike shape is not required.
        -- Such discontinuous closure is an AIC deduction, not a solution
        -- search. Continuous loops may expose subpaths with these outputs;
        -- no separate loop name or further inference rule is introduced.

    @guarantee BoundedForcingChains
        -- forcing_chain admits up to config.max_forcing_paths static-link
        -- paths, each of at most config.max_chain_links. No nested guesses
        -- and no path step that needs results of earlier nonadjacent steps.
        -- Contradiction: from one literal, paths reach incompatible literals
        -- (p and not-p; two positive digits in one cell; positive equal
        -- digits in peer cells;
        -- all candidates of a cell false; or every place for a unit's
        -- unplaced digit false). Negate the starting literal.
        -- Verity: roots exhaust both truth values of one candidate, all
        -- candidates of one cell, or every place of an unplaced unit digit.
        -- Every root must reach the same literal; assert that literal.
        -- A positive conclusion places, a negative conclusion strikes.
        -- An unfinished path or exhausted bound proves nothing. Logical
        -- case analysis is temporary, with no speculative board entry;
        -- writing a guess and playing its branch is a model action instead.

    @guarantee UniquenessPatterns
        -- Both require uniqueness_promised and, as DeductionsAreSound does
        -- of every technique, candidates that may be extra and are never
        -- missing: a stale candidate does not make either unsound.
        -- Unique Rectangle: the solution's digits at the four open corners
        -- are among their marks, so were the fourth corner a or b the
        -- corners would hold a and b alternately, and exchanging the two is
        -- a second solution whatever else is marked. BUG+1: in a unit the
        -- stale cell shares with the placed peer, two candidates to each
        -- open cell are exactly used up by two places to each unplaced
        -- digit, so a candidate for a placed digit cannot stand there and
        -- the count below fails. Were the removed candidate itself the
        -- stale one, what is left would be a true BUG, which a grid with one
        -- solution never reaches. Stale marks hide a BUG and never fake one.
        -- Unique Rectangle Type 1: four open cells occupy two rows, two
        -- columns and exactly two boxes. Three have exactly {a,b}; the
        -- fourth has both plus extras. Strike a,b from the fourth.
        -- No given is a corner; missing-candidate variants are excluded.
        -- BUG+1: every open cell is bivalue except one trivalue cell T.
        -- Removing candidate d from T must leave exactly two places for
        -- every unplaced digit in EVERY unit and two candidates in EVERY
        -- open cell. Place d in T. Checking T's units alone is insufficient.
        -- The proof includes the global check, not merely the shape of T.

    @guarantee ElementaryExamples
        -- These are acceptance fixture schemas. Complete unspecified cells
        -- consistently with the stated premises and at least one solution;
        -- confirm all solutions are preserved, not just a reference answer.
        -- Full unit: r1 has eight distinct placed digits except 9; its one
        -- open cell becomes 9. Two open cells reject that witness.
        -- Cross-hatch: for unplaced 7 in box1, placed peers exclude every
        -- open cell except r2c2; place 7 there. A second unexcluded cell
        -- rejects it even if r2c2 happens to be the solution.
        -- Naked single: r1c1={4} places 4; {4,8} rejects.
        -- Hidden single: only r1c1 allows 4 in row1, with {4,8}; place 4.
        -- Add 4 at another open row1 cell and that witness is rejected.
        -- Pointing: box1 has 7 only at r1c1,r1c2; strike it from r1c4.
        -- Add 7 at r2c3 and reject that row-pointing witness. A 7 already
        -- placed in box1 rejects it whatever the marks say.
        -- Claiming: row1 has 7 only at r1c1,r1c2; strike it from r2c3.
        -- Add 7 at r1c4 and reject that claiming witness. A 7 already
        -- placed in row1 rejects it whatever the marks say.
        -- Naked pair: {1,2},{1,2}; triple: {1,2},{2,3},{1,3};
        -- quad: {1,2},{2,3},{3,4},{1,4}, each in one unit.
        -- Strike their union from another cell. Add a new digit outside
        -- that union to a member and reject that specified k-cell witness.
        -- Hidden pair/triple/quad: respectively 2/3/4 digits have all their
        -- places in exactly that many cells, each digit appearing at least
        -- once; those cells also have 9. Strike 9 there. Add a place for a
        -- chosen digit outside those cells and reject that hidden witness.

    @guarantee AdvancedExamples
        -- As above, these schemas constrain exact witnesses, not an assertion
        -- that no OTHER technique holds after a negative mutation.
        -- X-Wing: d at c2,c7 only in base rows1,4; strike r8c2=d.
        -- Swordfish: rows1,4,7 have d respectively at c2c5,c5c8,c2c8;
        -- strike r9c5=d. Jellyfish: rows1,3,5,7 have d at c1c3,c3c5,
        -- c5c7,c1c7; strike r9c1=d. For each, add a candidate in a
        -- base outside its covers; that exact fish is no longer licensed.
        -- XY-Wing: r1c1={1,2}, r1c4={1,3}, r4c1={2,3};
        -- strike 3 at r4c4. Add 3 to pivot and the XY witness fails.
        -- XYZ-Wing: r1c1={1,2,3}, r1c2={1,3}, r2c1={2,3};
        -- strike 3 at r2c2. With wings instead at r1c4={1,3} and
        -- r2c2={2,3}, r2c4 sees both wings but not the pivot: no strike.
        -- W-Wing: P=r1c1,Q=r4c4 both {1,2}, conjugate
        -- 1 at r1c7,r4c7; strike 2 at r1c4. A third 1 in c7 rejects.
        -- Skyscraper: d only c1c5 in r1, c1c6 in r4; strike r2c6=d.
        -- A third base-line place rejects, as does d placed in a base
        -- line. Two-String Kite: d only r1c1,
        -- r1c5 in r1 and r2c2,r5c2 in c2; strike r5c5=d. Move the
        -- joined endpoints so they do not share a box: reject the witness.
        -- Empty Rectangle: d in box5 only r4c5,r4c6,r5c4; conjugate d
        -- at r4c1,r8c1 in c1; strike r8c4=d. Add r6c6=d: reject.
        -- Simple colouring: a connected conjugate path colours A and C
        -- alike; if A,C are peers that colour is struck. An outside target
        -- seeing opposite labels loses d; seeing only one label rejects.
        -- Remote Pair: {1,2} at r1c1,r1c4,r4c4,r4c2; strike 1,2 at
        -- r2c2. Add 3 to a chain cell and reject that chain.
        -- X-Chain: A=B-C=D for one digit, each '=' a conjugate link;
        -- target seeing A,D loses it. A weak first link rejects.
        -- XY-Chain: {1,2},{2,3},{3,4},{4,1} along consecutive peer
        -- cells; target seeing endpoints loses 1. A trivalue member rejects.
        -- AIC: A=B-C=D with mixed digits; any candidate weakly linked
        -- to A,D is struck. A candidate linked only to A is not struck.
        -- Forcing chain: complete paths for p and not-p both reach q;
        -- assert q. Omitting either path or exceeding the bound rejects.
        -- Unique Rectangle Type 1: r1c1,r1c2,r4c1={1,2},
        -- r4c2={1,2,3}; strike 1,2 at r4c2. Four boxes or no uniqueness
        -- premise rejects. BUG+1: removing 3 from the only {1,2,3}
        -- cell leaves the global BUG counts; place 3. Another trivalue
        -- cell, a failed count anywhere or no uniqueness premise rejects.

    @guarantee CatalogueValidation
        -- An implementation must instantiate every positive and near-miss
        -- schema above on full 81-cell candidate states, and check targets,
        -- proof premises, digit/row/column symmetry and solution preservation.
        -- Uniqueness fixtures require a verified unique original puzzle.
        -- An exact evaluator may validate fixtures; it may not supply the
        -- witness that a simulated player supposedly found.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Catalogue boundary
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Partial notes, general reasoning and speculative guesses are model
-- behaviour, not new catalogue entries. Deferred families are listed in
-- Excludes; expanding them requires new proof obligations and fixtures.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Resolved Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- This module once named nine techniques without specifying them and asked
-- nine questions. The catalogue as it now stands answers eight of them:
--   Placing by counting a cell's placed peers: no technique of its own.
--     Reconstructing the cell's candidates is a model's work and the final
--     singleton is naked_single, load one, with no free eight-digit chunk.
--   Refusing a reducible subset: no. A naked subset with a single-candidate
--     cell, or a hidden one with a smaller subset inside, stays licensed; a
--     systematic player takes the smaller pattern first by the ladder.
--   Fish: BasicFish. Finned and sashimi fish are deferred, in Excludes.
--   Wings: Wings. w_wing belongs beside xy_wing and xyz_wing.
--   simple_colouring: SimpleColouring. Its load is variable, below.
--   chain: several techniques and not one. skyscraper, two_string_kite,
--     empty_rectangle, remote_pair, x_chain, xy_chain and
--     alternating_inference_chain, by SingleDigitPatterns, RemotePairs and
--     AlternatingChains; grouped chains are deferred.
--   uniqueness_test: unique_rectangle_type_1 and bug_plus_one, by
--     UniquenessPatterns. A rating may lean on them and a hint may not: the
--     keeper states uniqueness_promised, true of givens sudoku.allium has
--     set and false of a puzzle in play whose entries nobody has verified.
--   nishio: a bounded contradiction test that writes nothing is a technique,
--     forcing_chain, by BoundedForcingChains. Writing a digit and playing
--     the branch is a guess and a model's. Unrestricted Nishio is deferred.
-- The ninth, partial marking, is answered only in human-solving.allium,
-- whose notes may be partial, and stands below for this module's Marking.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Partial marking, such as writing a digit only where it has two places in a box: is it a third Marking, and which techniques can read it? human-solving.allium's notes may be partial and claim coverage separately; the Profile's Marking is still none or full, so reach, effort and lapse cannot describe such a player."

open question "Loads and ladder order past subsets are provisional. A variable load counts the candidate propositions of the proof, which puts a long chain beyond every capacity Cowan suggests and so hides it from an expert in reach.allium and lapse.allium. Is that right, should a chain be held link by link, and is rank 13 to 29 the order a careful solver tries them in?"
